# estimai-api local environment (direnv).
#
# No 1Password-sourced secrets yet (specs/013-estimate-sharing, T13). The one
# secret this service now holds — NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN — follows the SAME
# local-dev posture already established by auth/.envrc, notify-api/.envrc,
# and refund-api/.envrc: it's a shared secret across what is now FOUR
# services (ADR-0011/ADR-0017/ADR-0040), but for local dev it's just a
# manually-generated value (`openssl rand -hex 32`) pasted into each
# service's own plain `.env` — none of the other three `.envrc` files fetch
# it via `op` either (see refund-api/.envrc's own note). Only the
# PRODUCTION Railway value is 1Password-sourced (infra/README.md § Variable
# reference: `AIScream / OperAI - NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN`) — wire estimai-api
# as a fourth Railway reader of that SAME item at deploy time, never a new
# vault item.
#
# Everything else this service reads (AUTH_BASE_URL, NOTIFY_INTERNAL_URL,
# SHARE_LOOKUP_FLOOR_MS, SHARE_ADD_RATE_LIMIT/_WINDOW_MS, plus the
# pre-existing AUTH_JWKS_URL/AUTH_ISSUER/AUTH_AUDIENCE/DATABASE_URL/
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS) is non-secret local config — see .env.example for the
# template (copy it to .env and fill in values, same as every other Operai
# backend).
#
# If this service ever gains a real 1Password-sourced secret, extend this
# file with the same pattern auth/.envrc uses: guard on a gitignored
# `.env.cached`, populate it with one `op read` per secret against the real
# vault item, then `source .env.cached`.
dotenv_if_exists .env
